t**To Reproduce**\n43\t\n44\tFor example, take this code:\n45\t\n46\t```python\n47\tassert (\n48\t    # my comment\n49\t    my_var == 999 if abc == \"TEST\" else 0\n50\t)\n51\t```\n52\t\n53\tBlack updates it to:\n54\t\n55\t```python\n56\tassert (\n57\t    # my comment\n58\t    my_var == 999\n59\t    if abc == \"TEST\"\n60\t    else 0\n61\t)\n62\t```\n63\t\n64\tThis introduces a bug:\n65\t- Original code: when `abc == \"TEST\"` this results in `assert my_var == 0`\n66\t- Updated code: when `abc == \"TEST\"` this results in `assert 0`\n67\t\n68\t**Expected \n69\t~~~~\n70\t\n71\t### candidate #5 (score 0.052, open, labels ['C: unstable formatting', 'F: fmtoff', 'T: bug'])\n72\ttitle: Unexpected error with Black 25.11.0 when fmt: off and fmt: on are used within block of comments\n73\t\n74\t~~~~ untrusted\n75\t<!--\n76\tPlease make sure that the bug is not already fixed either in newer versions or the\n77\tcurrent development version. To confirm this, you have three options:\n78\t\n79\t1. Update Black's version if a newer release exists: `pip install -U black`\n80\t2. Use the online formatter at <https://black.vercel.app/?version=main>, which will use\n81\t   the latest main branch.\n82\t3. Or run _Black_ on your machine:\n83\t   - create a new virtualenv (make sure it's the same Python version);\n84\t   - clone this repository;\n85\t   - run `pip install -e .[d]`;\n86\t   - run `pip install -r test_requirements.txt`\n87\t   - make sure it's sane by running `python -m pytest`; and\n88\t   - run `black` like you did last time.\n89\t-->\n90\t\n91\t**Describe the bug**\n92\t\n93\t<!-- A clear and concise description of what the bug is. -->\n94\t\n95\t**To Reproduce**\n96\t\n97\t<!--\n98\tMinimal steps to reproduce the behavior with source code and Black's configuration.\n99\t-->\n100\t\n101\tFor example, take this code:\n102\t\n103\t```python\n104\t[\n105\t    (1, 2),\n106\t    # # fmt: off\n107\t    # (3,\n108\t    #    4),\n109\t    # # fmt: on\n110\t    (5, 6),\n111\t]\n112\t```\n113\t\n114\tAnd run it with these arguments:\n115\t\n116\t```sh\n117\t$ black file.py\n118\t```\n119\t\n120\tThe resulting error is:\n121\t\n122\t> error: cannot format file.py: INTERNAL ERROR: Black 25.11.0 on Python (CPython) 3.11.13 produced different code on th\n123\t~~~~\n124\t\n125\t### candidate #6 (score 0.049, open, labels [])\n126\ttitle: Setting the 2026 stable style\n127\t\n128\t~~~~ untrusted\n129\t2023: upstream-issue-3407\n130\t2024: upstream-issue-4042\n131\t2025: upstream-issue-4522\n132\tRuff: https://github[.]com/astral-sh/ruff/issues/20482\n133\t\n134\tAs always, the ideal is for all preview features to be stabilized, not including the unstable ones.\n135\t\n136\t---\n137\t\n138\tOrdered by most controversial to least:\n139\t\n140\t<details>\n141\t<summary>multiline_string_handling (upstream-issue-1879)</summary>\n142\tMake expressions involving multiline strings more compact.\n143\t\n144\t**Input**\n145\t```py\n146\ttextwrap.dedent(\n147\t    \"\"\"\\\n148\t    This is a\n149\t    multiline string\n150\t\"\"\"\n151\t)\n152\t\n153\tMULTILINE = \"\"\"\n154\tfoobar\n155\t\"\"\".replace(\n156\t    \"\\n\", \"\"\n157\t)\n158\t```\n159\t\n160\t**Output (off)**\n161\t*unchanged*\n162\t\n163\t**Output (on)**\n164\t```py\n165\ttextwrap.dedent(\"\"\"\\\n166\t    This is a\n167\t    multiline string\n168\t\"\"\")\n169\t\n170\tMULTILINE = \"\"\"\n171\tfoobar\n172\t\"\"\".replace(\"\\n\", \"\")\n173\t```\n174\t\n175\tFairly controversial. There were some issues in upstream-issue-4159 that were never fully fixed. Additionally, it's only been in preview since v25.11 (but the issues were resolved as-best-as-possible since v25.9).\n176\tHowever, we haven't gotten any further feedback since then. I think the current state is an improvement overall and we probably won't be able to do much else.\n177\tIMO, we should at least include it in the first v26 beta to get user feedback, even if we don't plan on s\n178\t~~~~\n179\t\n180\t### candidate #1 (score 0.04, open, labels ['C: crash', 'C: preview style', 'F: fmtskip', 'T: bug'])\n181\ttitle: Crash with preview style `fix_fmt_skip_in_one_liners` for certain compound statements\n182\t\n183\t~~~~ untrusted\n184\tPlayground example below, but also reproduces with `async with` and `if` statements with compound boolean conditions (so perhaps related to upstream-issue-4731 but a priori does is not quite the same situation).\n185\t\n186\t**Black https://github[.]com/psf/black/commit/314f8c**\n187\t\n188\t[Playground link](https://black.vercel.app/?version=main&state=_Td6WFoAAATm1rRGAgAhARYAAAB0L-Wj4AC9AIBdAD2IimZxl1N_WmufDs0LpGEw9emYfCBl3bwWiaaPk-9VShmWdTZHtWIqHDNOo5vv3mtCRt2JPdYrP_88UxS71LUIZEYpZZ1dEE9hvybVY2-MCZ_Ou1Zl2ovnO0SjGtI8ifPSy2ScIBha4EzUEKo6liKlyiprVTWyJDh3AmX1byMAADAZ4agZ4L3QAAGcAb4BAADLeP5oscRn-wIAAAAABFla)\n189\t\n190\t## Options\n191\t\n192\t`--line-length=88`\n193\t`--safe`\n194\t`--preview`\n195\t\n196\t## Input\n197\t\n198\t```python\n199\twith open(\"file.txt\") as f: content = f.read() # fmt: skip\n200\t```\n201\t\n202\t## Output\n203\t\n204\t```python\n205\tCannot parse: 1:4: with\n206\t```\n207\t\n208\t## Expected\n209\t\n210\t```python\n211\twith open(\"file.txt\") as f: content = f.read() # fmt: skip\n212\t```\n213\t\n214\t~~~~\n215\t\n216\t\n217\t## ISSUE CONTENT (untrusted)\n218\t\n219\ttitle: Some people don't like lines with a single (or very few) characters\n220\t\n221\t~~~~~~~~ untrusted\n222\t**Describe the style change**\n223\t\n224\tMinimizing diffs is nice but this can come at the cost of reduced code readability.\n225\tNotably, when reading code on a monitor, there is only so much screen real estate.\n226\tSo the more lines the code is spread on, the more the user needs to scroll to read it and with the scrolling, the higher the cognitive load (need to remember parts that are not on screen).\n227\t\n228\tOne could argue that keeping brackets vertically aligned helps understand the code structure but modern IDEs already highlight this.\n229\t\n230\tIn the case of Black, there are many new lines with very few characters.\n231\tIt would be good to have an option to move these with an adjacent line.\n232\t\n233\tThe rules could be:\n234\t\n235\t- two options:\n236\t    - no-short-lines: bool, when true, this feature is activated\n237\t    - min-line-length: int\n238\t```\n239\tlines = sort(lines, len)  # sort lines by increasing length to process them by increasing length\n240\tfor line in lines:\n241\t    if len(line) < min-line-length and line[0] in (\"{\", \"}\", \"(\", \")\", \"[\", \"]\"):\n242\t        if the previous line has a training comma, remove it (no point)\n243\t        move line with the previous line\n244\t        if the previous line is now longer than max-line-length consider splitting it around a \":\"\n245\t```\n246\tThis does imply that max-line-length could be exceeded by min-line-length\n247\t\n248\t**Original code**\n249\tThese ten lines of code\n250\t```python\n251\tschema = Schema({\n252\t    Literal(\"a\", description=\"lorem ipsum\"):\n253\t        And([str], lambda x: len(x) >= 1 and len(x) <= 2),\n254\t    Literal(\"b\", description=\"lorem ipsum\"): And(str, len),\n255\t    Optional(\n256\t        Literal(\"c\", description=\"lorem ipsum lorem ipsum lorem ipsum lorem ipsum\"),\n257\t        default=\"\"): str,\n258\t    Optional(\n259\t        Literal(\"d\", description=\"lorem ipsum lorem ipsum lorem ipsum lorem ipsum\"),\n260\t        default=1): str,\n261\t    Optional(\n262\t        Literal(\"e\", description=\"lorem ipsum lorem ipsum lorem ipsum lorem ipsum\"),\n263\t        default=1): And(int, lambda x: x >= -1)},\n264\t    ignore_extra_keys=True,\n265\t    description=\"lorem ipsum\")\n266\t```\n267\t\n268\t**Examples in the current _Black_ style**\n269\tEnd up taking twenty lines\n270\t```python\n271\tschema = Schema(\n272\t    {\n273\t        Literal(\"a\", description=\"lorem ipsum\"): And(\n274\t            [str], lambda x: len(x) >= 1 and len(x) <= 2\n275\t     